WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.872
After detecting duplicates, define a survivor rule.

00:00:02.872 --> 00:00:07.252
A row with more useful fields might rank higher.

00:00:07.252 --> 00:00:12.762
A more recent record might be preferred when recency is meaningful.

00:00:12.762 --> 00:00:15.527
Some systems have a designated authoritative source.

00:00:15.527 --> 00:00:19.177
And ties should be broken deterministically.

00:00:19.177 --> 00:00:25.412
The important point is that survivor selection is a policy, not an automatic property of SQL.

00:00:25.412 --> 00:00:31.327
Duplicate resolution requires a definition of identity and a survivor rule.

00:00:31.327 --> 00:00:36.242
Exact duplicate rows are easy; duplicate people or customers are harder.

00:00:36.242 --> 00:00:42.327
If you choose one survivor, document the priority—latest update, verified source, highest completeness,

00:00:42.327 --> 00:00:47.452
or another defensible rule—and preserve links to the merged records.
